        <div class="guide-label">PipelineSync resource</div>
        <h2>What should a HubSpot governance checklist cover?</h2>
        <p class="guide-answer">A HubSpot governance checklist covers seven areas: access and permissions, the data model and properties, data quality and validation, naming conventions, automation, integrations, and reporting. Each item has an owner and a review rhythm, so the portal stays understandable and safe as more people and processes depend on it.</p>
        {#- TODO(owner): the resources page calls this a "Notion template". Publish the Notion copy and add the link in the paragraph below. -#}
        <p class="intro">Use this checklist before a new build, after a migration, or in a quarterly portal review. All 47 points are listed below, free and without a form.</p>

        <section id="access">
          <h3>Access and permissions (8)</h3>
          <ol class="gov-list" start="1"><li>Super admins limited to the people who own the portal</li><li>Permission sets defined per role</li><li>Teams match the real reporting structure</li><li>Export rights limited by role</li><li>Bulk delete rights limited by role</li><li>Departed users deactivated within a day</li><li>Records of departed users reassigned</li><li>Access tested with a normal user account, not an admin</li></ol>
        </section>
        <section id="data">
          <h3>Data model and properties (10)</h3>
          <ol class="gov-list" start="9"><li>Every custom property is in the property catalogue</li><li>Each property has an owner and a one-line definition</li><li>No duplicate properties for the same concept</li><li>Unused properties archived</li><li>Dropdowns used instead of free text where values repeat</li><li>Required properties set on forms and imports</li><li>Associations between objects documented</li><li>Record ID of source systems stored for imported data</li><li>Sensitive properties restricted to the roles that need them</li><li>Lifecycle stage and lead status definitions written down</li></ol>
        </section>
        <section id="validation">
          <h3>Data quality and validation (6)</h3>
          <ol class="gov-list" start="19"><li>Email, phone and country formats standardised</li><li>Company domains normalised</li><li>Duplicate review runs every week</li><li>Imports require a matching key</li><li>Only trained users can import</li><li>Data quality exceptions have a named owner</li></ol>
        </section>
        <section id="naming">
          <h3>Naming conventions (6)</h3>
          <ol class="gov-list" start="25"><li>Naming convention published for properties</li><li>Naming convention published for workflows</li><li>Naming convention published for lists and views</li><li>Naming convention published for reports and dashboards</li><li>Test and temporary assets clearly labelled</li><li>Old assets archived, not left active</li></ol>
        </section>
        <section id="automation">
          <h3>Automation (7)</h3>
          <ol class="gov-list" start="31"><li>Every workflow has a named owner</li><li>Every workflow has a one-line purpose</li><li>No two workflows set the same property in conflict</li><li>Workflow errors reviewed each week</li><li>Changes to live workflows tested on sample records first</li><li>Sequences and automated emails reviewed each quarter</li><li>A change log records what changed, when and why</li></ol>
        </section>
        <section id="integrations">
          <h3>Integrations (5)</h3>
          <ol class="gov-list" start="38"><li>Every connected app listed with its owner</li><li>Field ownership defined for each shared field</li><li>Sync errors alert a named person</li><li>Integrations update on a stable external ID</li><li>Unused apps disconnected</li></ol>
        </section>
        <section id="reporting">
          <h3>Reporting and review (5)</h3>
          <ol class="gov-list" start="43"><li>Dashboards leadership relies on are listed</li><li>Each key report has an owner who checks it</li><li>Report definitions match lifecycle and pipeline definitions</li><li>Unused dashboards archived</li><li>Full governance review held every quarter</li></ol>
        </section>

        <section id="use">
          <h2>How should you use this checklist?</h2>
          <p class="guide-answer">Assign each section to a named owner, mark every item as done, partly done or not done, and fix the not-done items in priority order: access first, then data, then automation. Re-run it every quarter and keep the results, so you can see whether the portal is getting healthier or drifting.</p>
          <div class="guide-callout"><p><strong>No internal CRM owner?</strong> Ongoing <a href="/solutions/hubspot-admin/">HubSpot administration</a> keeps governance running. <button type="button" class="text-link js-open-hs-form">Book a free consultation</button></p></div>
        </section>
        <div class="guide-callout"><p><strong>Key takeaway:</strong> Governance is not a pile of restrictions. It is the shared system that keeps a portal usable, explainable and safe as it grows.</p></div>
